 ## Changelog
 
+- 2026-11-04: Natural Temperature (Caesius_Leo, Fabric 1.2.1) ported, optional per world (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +4 tests: `gen::natural` x3, `menu` x1; `tools/natural_proto.py` was run against the mod's own classes: 6000 cases, 0 mismatches).
+    - What it is: the temperature and humidity of the Overworld laid out in bands / rings / squares round an equator (8 modes each: default, simplified, linear, circular, banded, circular6, square6, discrete) instead of in noise blobs, so the biomes follow like the Earth's. Read from the mod's bytecode (its licence allows reverse engineering); `world/gen/natural.rs` is `MathUtils`, `TemperatureCalculator`, `HumidityCalculator` and `PainterCore`.
+    - Proof: `tools/golden/NaturalHarness.java` runs the mod's own `PainterCore` on a JVM (class version patched 69 -> 65, Minecraft and Fabric stubbed, the two noises replaced by sine stubs) on random settings; `tools/natural_proto.py` (the Python twin the Rust mirrors) and the Rust test `matches_the_mods_own_classes` must give the same two numbers on `tools/golden/natural.txt` (168 cases, every mode). Mutating a constant of the twin breaks the check.
+    - Wiring: `NaturalSettings` is global while a world is open (`natural::set` in `enter_world`, `current()` in `WorldChunkManager::new`: the chunk workers, the far terrain and the spawn search all take it); `cell()` lays `Climate::sample` over the raw temp / humid noise at block (4 qx, 4 qz) (`influence` blends the two as the mod does). Off by default and when off nothing changes.
+    - UI: Create World has a "World-Gen Settings" button at its top right (`gen_ui`: the switch, the mode, every knob the mode reads, A = random / automatic for zones and transition points); the settings are saved in `<world>/natural` (none when default) and are shown read only from the game menu's Tools page.
+    - Not ported: `multidimensional_bands` (the Nether has no climate here), `randomize_underground` and `last_bedrock_y_level` (the climate is per column), the addon modes 101..105, Terrablender, the mod's own screen / button position.
+    - ponytail / UNVERIFIED: the two distortion noises are `chunk_manager::Normal` (the mod's `NormalNoise` on the game's Perlin: first octave -14 with amplitudes -14 x 3, and -7 with 1), so the bands wobble the same way in shape, not in the same places; the compile; the screen was never seen. Defaults are the mod's: `equatorial_distance` 20000 blocks a quarter, so a walk of a few thousand blocks stays in one zone; try 1500 to see the bands.
+    - ponytail: one world at a time (a global), upgrade: carry the `Arc<NaturalSettings>` down `WorldType`'s call chain.
 - 2026-10-24 (2): Structory (Stardust Labs, 1.20, FREETOUSE) in the Blooming world type only (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +4 tests in `gen::structory`; `tools/gen_structory.py` and `tools/structory_proto.py` were run).
     - Data: `python3 tools/gen_structory.py Structory-1_20.zip` writes `mc-rs/assets/structory.bin` (994 KB): 164 templates (the 238 `.nbt` minus those no pool names; `.schem` files are unused by the pools), 1258 block states each with its Beta (id, metadata), 6 processor lists resolved per state, 59 pools, 15 structures, 5 sets, 28 loot tables on Beta items. No NBT or inflate code in the game. The datapack's own holes are skipped with a warning: template `harvest/chapel/graveyard3`, the vanilla villager pools, pools `harvest/well_bottoms` and `harvest/old_manor/path`.
     - Placement (`Structory::populate`, last in `populate`, Blooming only): `random_spread` per set (spacing, separation, salt, triangular, `frequency`), the set's weighted pick with the fall-through to the next entry when the biome fails, the biome at the start chunk against the structure's tag (folded onto this world's 19 biomes in `BIOMES`, Blooming's woods standing for the forests, jungle, taiga, savanna, swamp and cherry grove they resemble), then the assembly: start piece centred on the chunk corner, rotation, `start_height` + ground, children by jigsaw name / target / joint, shuffled pools with fallback, the expansion hack, the max-distance area and per-parent free space. A start is a pure function of (seed, chunk), cached per generator; each populate writes the part of every piece inside its 16 x 16 cell, so nothing is saved and a structure that crosses cells comes out the same from every side.
